From: VyOS LS1046A Build <build@vyos.io>
Date: Tue, 01 Sep 2026 00:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: sfp: one-shot self-heal for OEM SFP-H10GB-CU1M
 phys_id defect

Both 10G SFP+ DAC modules on this DUT (vendor "OEM", part number
"SFP-H10GB-CU1M") fail EEPROM base-structure checksum validation on
every boot:

  sfp sfp-xfi0: EEPROM base structure checksum failure: 0xf0 != 0xf3
  sfp sfp-xfi1: EEPROM base structure checksum failure: 0xf0 != 0xf3

Board-verified byte-for-byte against a known-good unit of the exact
same part number on a different router: every byte of the base
structure is identical except offset 0 (phys_id / SFF-8472
"Identifier"), which reads 0x00 here instead of the correct 0x03
(SFP). The stored checksum byte itself (cc_base, offset 0x3F) is
untouched and correct (0xf3) on both the good and bad units -- only
phys_id was zeroed on this DUT's two modules, most likely by an
unrelated board-diagnostic script that performed a raw, unguarded
I2C write at address 0x50 (the universal SFP EEPROM address) without
pinning the target bus by device-tree phandle, so a bus-numbering
shift pointed it at these cages instead of its intended target.

Since mainline sfp.c treats any non-Cotsworks base checksum mismatch
as fatal (-EINVAL), this permanently fails the SFP state machine and
leaves the port at NO-CARRIER forever -- confirmed surviving both a
warm reboot and a full cold power-cycle.

Rather than just tolerating the mismatch, self-heal it: for this
specific known signature (this vendor/PN, phys_id==0x00, computed
check==0xf0, stored cc_base==0xf3 -- i.e. exactly and only the known
defect, nothing broader), write the correct phys_id (0x03) back to
the module's own EEPROM via the driver's existing sfp_write(), then
recompute the checksum against the corrected in-memory copy so this
same probe succeeds immediately rather than requiring a second boot.
Once written, the module's EEPROM is genuinely and permanently
corrected -- every future probe, on any kernel, sees a fully valid
checksum with no quirk or patch needed at all. This patch is
intentionally a one-shot fixup, not a permanent tolerance quirk: it
is expected to be removed from the series once confirmed to have
corrected both modules on hardware.

Signed-off-by: VyOS LS1046A Build <build@vyos.io>
---
 drivers/net/phy/sfp.c | 49 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 46 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c
@@ -2323,6 +2323,7 @@
 	struct sfp_eeprom_id id;
 	bool cotsworks_sfbg;
 	unsigned int mask;
+	bool oem_h10g_cu1m;
 	bool cotsworks;
 	u8 check;
 	int ret;
@@ -2388,6 +2389,21 @@
 			return ret;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * OEM SFP-H10GB-CU1M (unbranded 10G DAC): board-verified byte-for-byte
+	 * against a known-good unit of the same part number -- every base
+	 * structure byte is identical except offset 0 (phys_id), which reads
+	 * 0x00 here instead of the correct 0x03 (SFP). The stored checksum
+	 * byte (cc_base) is untouched at the correct 0xf3 either way; only
+	 * phys_id was zeroed. This is a one-shot self-heal: write the correct
+	 * phys_id back to the module's own EEPROM (so every future probe on
+	 * this module reads a fully valid, matching checksum with no driver
+	 * quirk needed at all), then continue this same probe with the
+	 * corrected in-memory copy rather than waiting for a second boot.
+	 */
+	oem_h10g_cu1m = !memcmp(id.base.vendor_name, "OEM             ", 16) &&
+			!memcmp(id.base.vendor_pn, "SFP-H10GB-CU1M", 14);
+
 	/* Validate the checksum over the base structure */
 	check = sfp_check(&id.base, sizeof(id.base) - 1);
 	if (check != id.base.cc_base) {
@@ -2395,6 +2411,32 @@
 			dev_warn(sfp->dev,
 				 "EEPROM base structure checksum failure (0x%02x != 0x%02x)\n",
 				 check, id.base.cc_base);
+		} else if (oem_h10g_cu1m && id.base.phys_id == 0x00 &&
+			   check == 0xf0 && id.base.cc_base == 0xf3) {
+			u8 fixed_phys_id = 0x03;
+			int wret = sfp_write(sfp, false, 0, &fixed_phys_id,
+					      sizeof(fixed_phys_id));
+
+			if (wret == sizeof(fixed_phys_id)) {
+				id.base.phys_id = fixed_phys_id;
+				check = sfp_check(&id.base, sizeof(id.base) - 1);
+				dev_warn(sfp->dev,
+					 "EEPROM base structure checksum failure (0x%02x != 0x%02x): known phys_id=0x00 defect, wrote corrected phys_id=0x03 to module EEPROM (one-shot self-heal, offset 0x00)\n",
+					 0xf0, 0xf3);
+			} else {
+				dev_err(sfp->dev,
+					"EEPROM self-heal write failed: %pe\n",
+					ERR_PTR(wret < 0 ? wret : -EIO));
+			}
+
+			if (check != id.base.cc_base) {
+				dev_err(sfp->dev,
+					"EEPROM base structure checksum failure: 0x%02x != 0x%02x\n",
+					check, id.base.cc_base);
+				print_hex_dump(KERN_ERR, "sfp EE: ", DUMP_PREFIX_OFFSET,
+					       16, 1, &id, sizeof(id), true);
+				return -EINVAL;
+			}
 		} else {
 			dev_err(sfp->dev,
 				"EEPROM base structure checksum failure: 0x%02x != 0x%02x\n",
